WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:03.330
- [Narrator] If you look
at every U.S. recession,

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.220
well, since World War II,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.740
you'll see two things always happen.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.340
The GDP, the measure of
economic output goes down

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.233
and unemployment goes up.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.610
- For most Americans, when
do you feel a recession?

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.310
It's when you start
worrying about your job.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.290
- [Narrator] Now let's look at today.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:26.970
The GDP is going down, even
the head of the federal reserve

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:27.960
says a recession is-

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.190
- Certainly a possibility.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.540
- [Narrator] But the unemployment rate,

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.280
it's actually falling.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.163
More people are getting
jobs, not losing them.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.490
- We don't know if it's a recession.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.340
What we can say is that if it is one,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.770
it's not like anything
we've ever seen before.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:44.880
- [Narrator] Here's why.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.120
The reason the GDP and
unemployment are always together

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.340
is because they feed on each other.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.250
When businesses layoff workers,
people spend less money

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.260
which means businesses make fewer profits

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.270
and lay off workers.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.180
A recession can begin
at any of these points.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.960
And in 2022, people could
cut back on spending.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.870
Just look at consumer
sentiment, a measure that weighs

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.660
how people are feeling about the economy

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.330
and whether they plan to spend money soon.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.340
Generally, when people
respond that they feel

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.640
more pessimistic, a recession follows.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.100
And right now they're
feeling about as good

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.450
as during the great recession
in 2008, thanks to inflation,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.640
not a great sign for the GDP.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.530
- If people are frustrated
by high inflation,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.350
if they think inflation is
gonna stick around for a while,

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.260
they might pull back convince
companies to cut back

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.170
and that leads to worse
outcomes for the economy.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.150
- [Narrator] But businesses
are in a different place

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.470
than past recessions.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.350
This chart shows the average
corporate profit margin.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.200
In the past profits were
in these single digits

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.040
leading into several
recessions including 2001.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.980
- That was a business driven
recession where companies

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.250
over-invested, they decided
they had to cut back

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.140
in order to rebuild our profit margins

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.060
and that led us right into a recession.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.350
- The bubble has now burst.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.200
I would not argue yet that
we've seen the bottom in NASDAQ

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.520
or in our tech index.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.680
- So are we back in one of those cycles?

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.300
In some ways it doesn't look like that

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.060
because profit margins
are so high right now.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.060
- [Narrator] Not only are
corporate profits in double digits

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:19.890
but the amount of cash they
have is close to $4 trillion.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.250
Analysts say that's a
significant downturn buffer.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.440
- They might just decide
they can weather this storm

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.190
and not lay off workers and
not cut back on investment

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.160
and kind of get us to the
other end of this adjustment

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.910
without a big retrenchment
that feeds on itself.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.100
- [Narrator] And companies
have an incentive to not lose

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.370
any workers because many are
having problems finding workers

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:42.810
in the first place.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.270
It's a really unique
time in the job market.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.160
The labor force participation rate

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.740
is as low as it's been in 40 years.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.960
And it isn't all due to the pandemic,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.690
the rate has been
declining for a long time.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.540
You can see it in the
unfilled job rates too.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.580
As more people leave the labor force,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.430
the more job positions become available.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:06.060
Add in a pandemic and
that's a lot of jobs.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.690
- 11 million jobs are out
there that they just can't find

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:10.560
people to fill.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.330
Now to put that in perspective,
if you look back at say 2019

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:18.810
when the economy was considered
to be exceptionally strong,

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.760
unfilled job openings were seven million.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.180
So like here we are talking
about a downturn in the economy

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.360
and we have four million more jobs open

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.630
than we had even at the
height of the last expansion.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.060
So you have to ask yourself
a question, why is that?

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.200
Why is that happening?

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.470
One answer is that there's
generational change happening

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.303
in the economy.

00:03:38.303 --> 00:03:40.950
We have millions of baby
boomers who are retiring.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.260
So there's just not as
many workers out there.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.140
It's also been the case that
there's just been really strong

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.540
demand for goods and services

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.730
in part because of all this
stimulus that was pumped

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.480
into the economy during the pandemic.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.050
And so that combination of labor
shortages and strong demand

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.990
created this really robust job market.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.850
- [Narrator] And it's
this robust job market

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:07.850
that can make a 2022 recession
if one develops so different.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.200
After past recessions,
there was what was called

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.950
jobless recoveries where
GDP began to grow back

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.500
but companies continued
to lay off workers.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.510
- [Jon] Right now we're
seeing a mirror image of that

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.370
where the economic output is contracting

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.260
and companies are still hiring.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.990
It's what I'm calling a job full downturn.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.570
- [Narrator] But how long
could an economic downturn

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.220
really be job full?

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.080
- This is unsustainable, right?

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.660
To have economic output contracting

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.550
at a time when companies are hiring.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.410
So one of two things has to happen.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.570
Either the economy adjusts

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.610
and companies start expanding again.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.490
Or the economy keeps contracting,

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:47.010
economic output keeps
contracting and companies say,

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.860
whoa, we're over our skis
and we've gotta cut jobs.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.750
One of those two things is gonna happen.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.730
We just don't know which of the two.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.440
- [Narrator] No recession has ever looked

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.850
exactly like another and if
there's a recession in 2022,

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.393
it won't look like anything
we've seen before either.

00:05:02.393 --> 00:05:05.476
(soft upbeat music)

